-- title:
--   Khare–Wintenberger: odd two-dimensional Artin representations of $\mathbb Q$
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $K$ is a number field, $G_K=\mathrm{Gal}(\bar K/K)$ its absolute Galois group with the Krull topology, and $\mathbb A_K$ its adele ring. An **Artin representation** is a continuous homomorphism $\rho: G_K\to\mathrm{GL}_n(\mathbb C)$. We say $\rho$ is **automorphic** if there are a nonzero bounded cusp form $\varphi$ on $\mathrm{GL}_n(\mathbb A_K)$ with unitary central character and a finite set $S$ of primes of $K$ such that for every prime $\mathfrak p\notin S$: $\rho$ is unramified at $\mathfrak p$, $\varphi$ is right invariant under $\mathrm{GL}_n(\mathcal O_{\mathfrak p})$, and
--   $$T_{\mathfrak p,i}\,\varphi \;=\; N\mathfrak p^{\,i(n-i)/2}\,e_i\big(\rho(\mathrm{Frob}_{\mathfrak p})\big)\,\varphi\qquad(1\le i\le n),$$
--   where $T_{\mathfrak p,i}$ is the Hecke operator of the double coset of $\mathrm{diag}(\varpi,\dots,\varpi,1,\dots,1)$ ($i$ entries $\varpi$) and $e_i(\rho(\mathrm{Frob}_{\mathfrak p}))$ is the $i$-th elementary symmetric function of the eigenvalues of an arithmetic Frobenius. Equivalently, the cuspidal automorphic representation generated by $\varphi$ has the same local $L$-factors as $\rho$ at all $\mathfrak p\notin S$.
--
--   **Theorem.** Let $\rho:\mathrm{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)\to\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb C)$ be a continuous irreducible representation which is **odd**, i.e. $\det\rho(c)=-1$ for every complex conjugation $c$. Then $\rho$ is automorphic; classically, $\rho$ is attached to a weight-one cuspidal newform.
--
--   This is a consequence of Serre's modularity conjecture, proved by Khare and Wintenberger, combined with the Deligne–Serre theorem. It settles the reciprocity conjecture for odd two-dimensional representations of $\mathbb Q$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** By the Artin–Schreier theorem, the elements of order $2$ in $\mathrm{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ are exactly the complex conjugations, i.e. the restrictions of complex conjugation along embeddings $\bar{\mathbb Q}\hookrightarrow\mathbb C$. Oddness is therefore stated as: $\det\rho(c)=-1$ for every $c\neq 1$ with $c^2=1$.
-- source:
--   C. Khare and J.-P. Wintenberger, Serre's modularity conjecture (I), Invent. Math. 178 (2009), 485-504, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-009-0205-7

import Definitions.Def_LanglandsReciprocity_defs
import Mathlib

namespace LanglandsReciprocity

open NumberField

/-- **Milestone (Khare–Wintenberger: odd two-dimensional over `ℚ`).** An irreducible
two-dimensional Artin representation of `ℚ` which is odd — `det ρ(c) = -1` for every complex
conjugation `c`, i.e. every element of order `2` of `Gal(ℚ̄/ℚ)` — is automorphic. -/
theorem reciprocity_two_rat_odd
    (ρ : Gal(AlgebraicClosure ℚ/ℚ) →* GL (Fin 2) ℂ) (hρ : Continuous ρ)
    (hirr : IsIrreducible ρ)
    (hodd : ∀ c : Gal(AlgebraicClosure ℚ/ℚ), c ≠ 1 → c * c = 1 →
      (ρ c : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ).det = -1) :
    IsAutomorphic ρ := by sorry

end LanglandsReciprocity
